<Layout title="Story" description="The story of Signal Cycles, 2007–2017.">
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>The Signal Cycles story</h1>
  </section>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <em>
        Draft copy — pulled together from Matt's blog post about Signal Cycles.
        Rewrite freely; this is just a starting point.
      </em>
    </p>
    <p>
      Signal Cycles started in the fall of 2007, in a small garage in North Portland.
      Matt Cardinal and Nate Meschke, two painters who'd met working at Bike Gallery,
      decided to try building bicycle frames by hand. Neither of them had done it
      before. Nate had bike shop experience and a fine art degree in painting; he'd
      moved to Portland from Nebraska with his wife, Emily.
    </p>
    <p>
      By February of 2008, four months after cutting their first tubes, they debuted
      four bikes at the North American Handmade Bicycle Show in Portland: a 29-inch
      singlespeed mountain bike, a 700c city bike with custom racks, a geared
      cyclocross bike, and a lugged road bike. They took three orders that weekend.
    </p>
    <p>
      Over the next decade, Signal built custom frames one at a time — road bikes,
      city bikes, cyclocross bikes, even a mountain bike for a 7-foot-tall Trail
      Blazers player. In 2011 the shop moved to North Page Street, alongside other
      Portland framebuilders, and won Best City Bike at NAHBS in Austin. That same
      year, Signal collaborated with Anthropologie on the "Picnic Bike," a
      step-through frame with a custom front rack and wicker basket, and worked with
      Ziba Design on an entry for the Oregon Manifest utility bike competition — a
      project that would later steer Matt toward a career in industrial design.
    </p>
    <p>
      Signal also ran a small production line — the Saltzman and the Pulse — to make
      the business more sustainable than pure custom work allowed, and sponsored a
      grassroots cyclocross team for a few seasons. Bikes went out to Japan, China,
      Indonesia, and across Europe and the U.S.
    </p>
    <p>
      Matt left full-time work at Signal in 2013 to pursue a master's degree in
      industrial design in Savannah, Georgia. Nate kept building. The last Signal
      frame shipped in the spring of 2018.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site exists to keep a record of what they built: a gallery of the bikes
      that came out of that shop, in earthy tones and careful lugwork, made by two
      people who cared more about the work than the margins.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
